'''Acronuridæ''' (ak-rọ̄-nū′ri-dē), ''n. pl.'' [< ''Acronurus'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of spiny-finned fishes, referred by Günther to his ''Acanthopterygii cottoscombriformes'', having one dorsal with several spongy spines anteriorly, one or more bony spines on each side of the tail, and the teeth compressed, truncate or lobate, and closely set in a single series. {{smaller|The species are known as ''barber-fish'' and ''surgeons''. The family is also called ''Acanthuridæ'' and ''Teuthididæ''. See these words.}}

'''Acronurus''' (ak-rọ̄-nū′rus), ''n.'' [NL., appar. irreg. < Gr. ἄκρον, extremity, + οὐρά, tail.] A former generic name of small fishes now known to be the young of species of ''Acanthurus'' (which see).

'''acronych''' (a-kron′ik), ''a.'' [Also written ''acronyc'', ''acronic'', and ''achronic'', by confusion with adjectives in ''-ic'' and with ''chronic'' and Gr. χρόνος, time; = F. ''acronyque'' = Sp. ''acronicto'', ''acrónico'' = Pg. ''acronico'', ''achronico'' = It. ''acronico'', < Gr. ἀκρόνυχος, also ἀκρόνυκτος and ἀκρονύκτιος, at nightfall, < ἄκρος, at the end or edge, + νύξ (νυκτ-) = E. ''night''.] Same as ''acronychal''.

'''acronychal''' (a-kron′i-kạl), ''a.'' [Also written ''acronycal'', ''acronical'', etc., as ''acronych''; < ''acronych'' + ''-al''.] In ''astron.'', occurring at sunset: as, the ''acronychal'' rising or setting of a star: opposed to ''cosmical''.—{{smaller|'''Acronychal place''' or '''observation''', the place or observation of a planet at its opposition: so called because in an early state of astronomy the opposition of a planet was known by its acronychal rising.}}
